<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hades</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Plouton, Pluto, Aïdōneús</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hades is, in ancient Greek religion and mythology, the god of the dead and of riches and the king of the underworld, with which his name became synonymous. The eldest son of Cronus and Rhea, he and his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> overthrew the Titans and shared the cosmos, Hades receiving the underworld, Zeus the sky and Poseidon the sea. His most important myth is the abduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>, told in the Homeric Hymn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> of most likely the early 6th century BCE: having eaten a pomegranate seed, she was to spend a third of each year with him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of around the 5th century BCE began to call him Plouton, and Roman mythographers merged him with Dis Pater and Orcus as Pluto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hades.jpg" alt="Hades" width="330" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Template:Peintre Codrus · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persephone_Hades_BM_Vase_E82_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The royal tombs at Vergina, with murals of <b>Hades</b> abducting Persephone, golden coffins and arms, and the bronze Derveni Krater show an elite that imported Greek artistic traditions while keeping older funerary rites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hades</b> ruled the underworld with Persephone.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Carried west by Phoenician sailors, Canaanite ideas can be seen in Greek mythology: the division of the world between Zeus, Poseidon and <b>Hades</b> mirrors that between Baal, Yam and Mot, and the Labours of Hercules mirror the stories of Tyre’s Melqart, who was often equated with Heracles.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer's Odyssey placed the Cimmerians in a sunless land at the entrance of <b>Hades</b>, a poetic image that later writers moved to Italy, the far north or Hyperborea, and Posidonius equated them with the Cimbri.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>They call Christ the pre-existent Word, the Son of Man and the Son of God, speak of Father, Son and Holy Spirit, allude to the crucifixion and the descent into <b>Hades</b>, and, depending on their date, may hold the earliest attestation of the virgin birth outside the Bible.</blockquote><a href="/songs/odes-of-solomon">Odes of Solomon</a></li></ul></section>
